WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:06.120
Welcome back, in
the last video you

2
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.330
learned about the depth-wise
separable convolution.

3
00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.610
Let's now put this
into a neural network

4
00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:14.130
in order to build the MobileNet.

5
00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:17.820
The idea of MobileNet
is everywhere that

6
00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.440
you previously have used

7
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.885
an expensive
convolutional operation.

8
00:00:21.885 --> 00:00:23.715
You can now instead use

9
00:00:23.715 --> 00:00:25.635
a much less expensive

10
00:00:25.635 --> 00:00:28.995
depthwise separable
convolutional operation,

11
00:00:28.995 --> 00:00:33.225
comprising the depthwise
convolution operation

12
00:00:33.225 --> 00:00:36.645
and the pointwise
convolution operation.

13
00:00:36.645 --> 00:00:41.145
The MobileNet v1 paper had
a specific architecture

14
00:00:41.145 --> 00:00:45.825
in which it use a block
like this, 13 times.

15
00:00:45.825 --> 00:00:49.985
It would use a depthwise
convolutional operation

16
00:00:49.985 --> 00:00:53.690
to genuine outputs and
then have a stack of 13 of

17
00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:56.120
these layers in order to go from

18
00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.835
the original raw input image to

19
00:00:58.835 --> 00:01:02.675
finally making a
classification prediction.

20
00:01:02.675 --> 00:01:07.565
Just to provide a few more
details after these 13 layers,

21
00:01:07.565 --> 00:01:10.340
the neural networks
last few layers

22
00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:12.635
are the usual Pooling layer,

23
00:01:12.635 --> 00:01:15.275
followed by a fully
connected layer,

24
00:01:15.275 --> 00:01:17.650
followed by a Softmax

25
00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.845
in order for it to make a
classification prediction.

26
00:01:20.845 --> 00:01:23.105
This turns out to perform well

27
00:01:23.105 --> 00:01:25.340
while being much
less computationally

28
00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:28.280
expensive than earlier algorithms

29
00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.550
that used a normal
convolutional operation.

30
00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:34.340
In this video, I want
to share with you

31
00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:36.260
one more improvements on

32
00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:39.075
this basic MobileNet
architecture,

33
00:01:39.075 --> 00:01:42.360
which is the MobileNets
v2 architecture.

34
00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:43.850
I'm going to go through this

35
00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:45.200
quite quickly to just give you

36
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.790
a rough sense of
what the algorithm

37
00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:49.235
does without digging
into all of the details.

38
00:01:49.235 --> 00:01:52.160
But this is a paper published
by Mark Sandler and

39
00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.800
his colleagues that make
mobile network even better.

40
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:59.580
In MobileNet v2, there
are two main changes.

41
00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:03.525
One is the addition of
a residual connection.

42
00:02:03.525 --> 00:02:05.930
This is just a residual
connections that you

43
00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:08.595
learned about in
the ResNet videos.

44
00:02:08.595 --> 00:02:11.330
This residual connection
or skip connection,

45
00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:14.255
takes the input from
the previous layer

46
00:02:14.255 --> 00:02:18.095
and sums it or passes it
directly to the next layer,

47
00:02:18.095 --> 00:02:19.640
does allow ingredients to

48
00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:22.055
propagate backward
more efficiently.

49
00:02:22.055 --> 00:02:27.170
The second change is that it
also as an expansion layer,

50
00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:30.280
which you learn more
about on the next slide,

51
00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:33.380
before the depthwise convolution,

52
00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:35.915
followed by the
pointwise convolution,

53
00:02:35.915 --> 00:02:37.240
which we're going to call

54
00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.310
projection in a
point-wise convolution.

55
00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:40.715
Is really the same operation,

56
00:02:40.715 --> 00:02:42.200
but we'll give it
a different name,

57
00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.750
for reasons that you
see on the next slide.

58
00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:46.610
What MobileNet v2 does,

59
00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:48.080
is it uses this block and

60
00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:50.315
repeats this block
some number of times,

61
00:02:50.315 --> 00:02:53.340
just like we had 13 times here,

62
00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.520
the MobileNet v2
architecture happened to

63
00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:57.905
choose to do this 17 times,

64
00:02:57.905 --> 00:03:01.310
surpass the inputs through
17 of these blocks,

65
00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:03.125
and then finally ends up with

66
00:03:03.125 --> 00:03:06.920
the usual pooling
fully-connected softmax,

67
00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:09.710
in order to make a
classification prediction.

68
00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:14.000
But the key idea is really
how blocks like this,

69
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.540
or like this reduces
computational cost.

70
00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:24.225
This block over here is also
called the bottleneck block.

71
00:03:24.225 --> 00:03:26.490
Let's dig into the details of how

72
00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:29.730
the MobileNet v2 block works.

73
00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:31.880
Given an input that is say,

74
00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:35.435
n by n by three,

75
00:03:35.435 --> 00:03:38.990
the MobileNet v2
bottleneck will pass

76
00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:41.660
that input via the
residual connection

77
00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:43.550
directly to the output,

78
00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:45.115
just like in the Resnet.

79
00:03:45.115 --> 00:03:47.220
Then in this main

80
00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:50.085
non-residual connection
parts of the block,

81
00:03:50.085 --> 00:03:54.115
you'll first apply an
expansion operator,

82
00:03:54.115 --> 00:03:56.670
and what that means
is you'll apply a

83
00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:59.570
1 by 1 by n c. In this case,

84
00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:02.315
one by one by
three-dimensional filter.

85
00:04:02.315 --> 00:04:04.220
But you apply a fairly
large number of

86
00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:06.920
them, say 18 filters,

87
00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.605
so that you end up with an n by

88
00:04:09.605 --> 00:04:13.850
n by 18-dimensional
block over here.

89
00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:16.850
A factor of expansion of six is

90
00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:19.520
quite typical in
MobileNet v2 which is

91
00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:21.920
why your inputs goes from n

92
00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:25.299
by n by three to n by n by 18,

93
00:04:25.299 --> 00:04:27.490
and that's why we call
it an expansion as well,

94
00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:31.295
it increases the dimension
of this by a factor of six,

95
00:04:31.295 --> 00:04:36.980
the next step is then a
depthwise separable convolution.

96
00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:40.150
With a little bit of padding,

97
00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:45.735
you can then go from n by n
by 18 to the same dimension.

98
00:04:45.735 --> 00:04:49.290
In the last video, we
went from 6 by 6 by 3

99
00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:52.555
to 4 by 4 by 3 because
we didn't use padding.

100
00:04:52.555 --> 00:04:54.700
But with padding, you
can maintain the n

101
00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:57.104
by n by 18 dimension,

102
00:04:57.104 --> 00:04:58.500
so it doesn't shrink when you

103
00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:02.010
apply the depthwise convolution.

104
00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:07.285
Finally, you apply a
pointwise convolution,

105
00:05:07.285 --> 00:05:10.500
which in this case means
convolving with a 1 by

106
00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:14.475
1 by 18-dimensional filter.

107
00:05:14.475 --> 00:05:16.995
If you have, say,

108
00:05:16.995 --> 00:05:21.625
three filters and
c prime filters,

109
00:05:21.625 --> 00:05:25.930
then you end up with
an output that is n by

110
00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:30.935
n by 3 because you have
three such filters.

111
00:05:30.935 --> 00:05:32.655
In this last step,

112
00:05:32.655 --> 00:05:37.425
we went from n by n by 18
down to n by n by three,

113
00:05:37.425 --> 00:05:40.680
and in the MobileNet
v2 bottleneck block.

114
00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:44.375
This last step is also
called a projection

115
00:05:44.375 --> 00:05:47.655
step because you're
projecting down from

116
00:05:47.655 --> 00:05:51.270
n by n by 18 down to n by n by 3.

117
00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:53.490
You might be wondering, why do we

118
00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:56.415
meet these bottleneck blocks?

119
00:05:56.415 --> 00:05:59.365
It turns out that
the bottleneck block

120
00:05:59.365 --> 00:06:02.085
accomplishes two things, One,

121
00:06:02.085 --> 00:06:04.490
by using the expansion operation,

122
00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:06.080
it increases the size of

123
00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:09.705
the representation within
the bottleneck block.

124
00:06:09.705 --> 00:06:11.540
This allows the neural network

125
00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:13.265
to learn a richer function.

126
00:06:13.265 --> 00:06:16.970
There's just more
computation over here.

127
00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:20.450
But when deploying
on a mobile device,

128
00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:22.490
on edge device, you will

129
00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:24.770
often be heavy
memory constraints.

130
00:06:24.770 --> 00:06:29.450
The bottleneck block uses
the pointwise convolution or

131
00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:32.660
the projection operation in
order to project it back

132
00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:36.540
down to a smaller set of values,

133
00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:39.050
so that when you pass
this the next block,

134
00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:41.120
the amount of memory needed to

135
00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:44.000
store these values is
reduced back down.

136
00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.520
The clever idea, The
cool thing about

137
00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:48.200
the bottleneck block is that it

138
00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:50.450
enables a richer set
of computations,

139
00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:52.370
thus allow your neural
network to learn

140
00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:54.349
richer and more
complex functions,

141
00:06:54.349 --> 00:06:58.280
while also keeping the amounts
of memory that is the size

142
00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:00.080
of the activations
you need to pass from

143
00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:02.330
layer to layer, relatively small.

144
00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:04.730
That's why the
MobileNet v2 can get

145
00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:07.880
a better performance
than MobileNet v1,

146
00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:10.400
while still continuing to use

147
00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:14.560
only a modest amount of
compute and memory resources.

148
00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:17.235
Putting It all together again,

149
00:07:17.235 --> 00:07:21.885
here's our earliest slide
describing MobileNet v1 and v2.

150
00:07:21.885 --> 00:07:24.620
The MobileNet v2
architecture will

151
00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:28.899
repeat this bottleneck
block 17 times.

152
00:07:28.899 --> 00:07:33.470
Then if your goal is to
make a classification,

153
00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:35.090
then as usual will have

154
00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:40.625
this pooling fully-connected
softmax layer

155
00:07:40.625 --> 00:07:44.315
to generate the
classification output.

156
00:07:44.315 --> 00:07:48.715
You now know the main ideas
of MobileNet v1 and v2.

157
00:07:48.715 --> 00:07:52.940
If you want to see a few
additional details such as

158
00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:55.280
the exact dimensions of
the different layers as

159
00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:58.025
you flow the data
from left to right.

160
00:07:58.025 --> 00:08:00.320
You can also take a look at

161
00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:03.085
the paper by Mark Sandler
and others as well.

162
00:08:03.085 --> 00:08:05.780
Congrats on making it to
the end of this video,

163
00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:09.680
you now know how MobileNet
v1 and v2 work by using

164
00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:12.830
depthwise separable
convolutions and by

165
00:08:12.830 --> 00:08:16.055
using the bottleneck blocks
that you saw in this video.

166
00:08:16.055 --> 00:08:18.350
If you are thinking of

167
00:08:18.350 --> 00:08:20.765
building efficient
neural networks,

168
00:08:20.765 --> 00:08:24.515
there's one other idea
to found very useful,

169
00:08:24.515 --> 00:08:27.440
and I hope you will
learn about as well,

170
00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:28.930
which is efficient nets.

171
00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:33.090
Let's touch on that idea very
briefly in the next video.